# Article: Handoffs

Handoffs are the only way work moves between roles. The helper scripts are
the only way you touch them.

## Sending

1. Write a draft file (e.g. `tmp/handoff.txt`) containing headers only.
2. Run `swarmforge/scripts/swarm_handoff.sh <draft-file>`.
3. On success it prints `HANDOFF QUEUED:` and deletes your draft. On
   failure it lists every problem; repair the draft and rerun.

Two draft formats exist, and only these two:

    type: git_handoff
    to: <role>[,<role>...]
    priority: NN
    task: <short-stable-task-name>
    commit: <10-char-commit-abbrev>

    type: note
    to: <role>[,<role>...]
    priority: NN
    message: <one line, max 80 chars>

## Rules

- `git_handoff` is for committed work. Get the 10-character abbreviation
  with `git rev-parse --short=10 HEAD`.
- The `task` name follows one piece of work through the whole chain: when
  forwarding work you received, keep its task name; when starting new work,
  coin a new short stable name.
- `priority` is `00` (most urgent) through `99`. Use `50` unless the pack
  article or the user says otherwise; everything at one priority is what a
  batch-mode role consumes as one batch.
- `note` is rare. Send one only when the user, your role prompt, or the
  constitution explicitly directs it — never as a substitute for stopping
  and asking when blocked.
- Never write the reserved headers (`id`, `from`, `role`, `recipient`,
  `created_at`, `enqueued_at`, `dequeued_at`, `completed_at`); the tooling
  owns them.
- Never create, edit, move, or delete anything under
  `.swarmforge/handoffs/` yourself, and never commit that directory. The
  scripts own all queue state.
